Running bare yoyo watch in a directory where it can't detect a lint or test command now exits 2, the usage-error code. It used to exit 0. The printed text hasn't changed. It's committed as 2b5a5c17 and pushed to main, and #982's body records the fix.

What changed
• `src/watch.rs`: the watch handler now returns a status code. It returns 2 only when bare watch or watch all finds nothing to run, and 0 on every other path. The interactive /watch command ignores the code, so it never exits the process.
• `src/dispatch_sub.rs`: the shell watch command now exits with that code, the same way def and outline already do.
• `docs/src/usage/commands.md`: the exit-codes paragraph now describes this case.

Checks
• New test: it calls the status function directly, since calling the exiting command in-process would kill the test binary. It checks four things:
  • An empty temp directory returns 2 for both /watch and /watch all, and no watch command gets set.
  • A temp directory with a Cargo.toml returns 0.
  • status, off and an explicit command return 0 even in the empty directory.
  • Detection really finds nothing in the empty directory, so the test can't pass by accident.
• Sabotage check: I temporarily made the empty-directory case return 0, and the new test failed by name. After restoring it, the test passed again (the temporary change was restored in the same command).
• Built binary:
  • Empty directory: the "Specify one" text appears and it exits 2.
  • Directory with a Cargo.toml: exits 0.
  • watch make check and watch status: both exit 0.
• cargo build, cargo test (all suites passed), cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings and cargo fmt -- --check all passed.

#982's body: I added an entry to its "Already fixed" paragraph, keyed by the no-command-detected condition rather than by the verb. It also names one more case of the same bug that I left unfixed: yoyo watch lint with no lint command detected prints its own "No lint command detected" usage text and still exits 0. I found that by reading the code and haven't run it. The commit message names it too.

The commit says "Part of #982", not "Fixes", because the issue still has many members open.
